  "query_preview": "..A weak ridge will start to break down as it \nmoves to the east today. The southerly flow around the backside of \nthe ridge will allow for better moisture advection across the region \ntoday. The increasing moisture will result in cloudy skies for much \nof the region but with better lift and a slight increase in shear we \nwill also see isolated to scattered showers with a few thunderstorms \ndevelop this afternoon, continuing into the early evening. These \nshowers will be over eastern Arizona, mainly to the east of the I-17 \ncorridor and more likely over the higher terrain. Southwesterly \nwinds will be lighter today than the past couple of days with speeds \ngenerally around 10-20 mph across the region. \n\nThe weak ridge slides to the east by early Tuesday as a deepening \nPacific low starts to make its way eastward. The tightening pressure \ngradient between the approaching low and departing ridge will \nincrease the southwesterly winds across the region. Expect winds \naround 15-25 mph with gusts to 30-35 mph on Tuesday, increasing to \n20-30 mph on Wednesday with gusts to 40-45 mph. A Wind Advisory may \nbe needed for parts of the area on Wednesday but as it is marginal \nat this point, we will hold off issuing one at this time. Cooler air \nwill filter into the region for the middle of the week.\n\nThe low tracks into northern California and then passes through the \nGreat Basin adn into Utah, remaining north of our area. Latest model \nruns continue to keep the forecast area dry as the low passes to our \nnorth. Any moisture than can find its way into our area as a result \nof the low will be limited and there may only be a few very isolated \nshowers across far northern Arizona on Wednesday with most areas \nremaining dry.\n\nBehind the departing low, we should see the flow turn more zonal for \nthe rest of the week with a return to seasonably warm temperatures, \nbreezy afternoon winds, and dry conditions. Several shortwaves may \nmove through the region next weekend and into early next week with \nthe chance for some showers to return to the region.",
